--- !u!114 &860390008
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 860390003}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Legend of Maria Makiling\n\nOnce upon a time, when the earth was still
    young and the mountains still whispered to the skies, there lived a diwata on
    a mountain in Laguna. Her name was Maria Makiling. \n\nShe was incredibly beautiful\u2014her
    skin the color of light olive leaves kissed by the sun, her long, flowing black
    hair flowing like rivers of silk down her back, and her eyes were always twinkling
    as if she held the secrets of the universe.\n\nBut it was not only her beauty
    that captured the heart of the people. \n\nMaria was generous, and her heart
    beat with kindness. She had a special liking for the people who lived in the
    village beneath her mountain.\n\nOne day, a humble farmer came to her, eyes full
    of worry. His children were gravely ill, and he had nothing left to offer. \n\nWithout
    hesitation, Maria smiled gently and handed him a bilao filled with ordinary ginger.\n\nThe
    man bowed low in gratitude, though his heart remained heavy. But as he walked
    home, the ginger began to glow. \n\nBy the time he arrived, each piece had turned
    into solid gold. The farmer wept\u2014not from sadness, but from wonder. \n\nNews
    of Maria\u2019s gift spread quickly, and the townsfolk loved her all the more
    for it.\n\nAs time passed, many suitors wished for Maria\u2019s hand. She was
    so enchanting, many men climbed the mountain trails just to catch a glimpse of
    her.\n\nThree were especially determined: \n\n\nKapitan Lara, a Spanish soldier
    who brought her gifts from Europe; \n\t\nJoselito, a Spanish mestizo student
    from Manila who told her stories about foreign lands and dreamed of Spain, \n\nand
    Juan, a common farmer who was very hard-working. His fruits and vegetables grew
    big and juicy, and he had many pet animals and birds.\n\nThough she said nothing,
    her eyes lingered a little longer on Juan each time he visited.\n\nThe suitors,
    however, grew relentless.\n\n\u201CChoose one of us,\u201D they demanded.\n\nMaria,
    ever graceful, replied with a nod. \u201CBy the next full moon, I will give my
    answer.\u201D\n\nWhen the silver moon rose in the sky, casting a glow upon the
    peak of her mountain, the three men climbed to hear her decision.\n\nThere, beneath
    the stars, Maria spoke: \u201CI choose Juan.\u201D\n\nHer voice echoed down the
    mountainside. Juan\u2019s eyes widened in disbelief, and then joy. But Kapitan
    Lara and Joselito stood frozen, their prides shattered.\n\nThey left in silence\u2014but
    their hearts burned with envy.\n\nThey grew angry, so angry, that they planned
    something evil against Juan as revenge.\n \n  \nSome weeks later, a fire was
    spotted inside the Spanish cuartel. The flames reached high into the night sky,
    and chaos consumed the town.\n\nKapitan Lara used the moment. Using fear as his
    weapon, he ordered many arrests\u2014rounding up countless innocent Filipinos.\n\nIn
    the shadows, Joselito helped him using soft words and softer lies.\n\nAmong them\u2026
    was Juan.\n\nInside the overcrowded prison, despair settled like smoke. Many
    were beaten, starved, and left to suffer.\n\nKapitan Lara and Joselito, in their
    pain, planted a lie\u2014that Juan had caused the fire.\n\nThat night, under
    the cover of darkness, they went into the prison.\n\n\u201CIf you want the torture
    to stop,\u201D Joselito whispered to the scared prisoners, \u201Ctell the guards
    Juan started the fire.\u201D  \n\n\nThe prisoners felt fear. And when the guards
    came to question them, no one dared to tell the truth.\n\n\u201CI didn\u2019t
    do it!\u201D Juan shouted as the soldiers dragged him into the plaza.\n\nBut
    he was met with silence. No prisoner spoke up. \n\nBy dawn, the verdict was clear\u2014Juan
    was declared a traitor.\n\nIn front of hundreds, he was tied up and forced to
    kneel. The Spanish soldiers raised their rifles, ready to shoot an innocent man.\n\nBefore
    the bullets flew, Juan cried out with all his strength:\n\n\u201CMaria!\u201D\n\nFrom
    her mountain, the diwata heard Juan\u2019s cry.\n\nShe rushed down, her feet
    barely touching the earth. But when she reached the plaza, it was too late. Juan
    had already died.\n\nWith trembling hands, she knelt beside him, holding his
    lifeless body, and cried\u2014not just for her lover, but for the cruelty of
    a world that silenced love.\n\nShe stood, her face no longer gentle, but in anger.\n\n\u201CWhy
    did none of you protect him?\u201D she cried to the people.\n\nNo one answered.
    No one could.\n\n\nWord reached her that Joselito and Kapitan Lara were the cause
    of this and had fled to Manila. \n\nMaria raised her arms to the heavens and
    cursed them\u2014and all men who had an evil heart.\n\nNot long after, her curse
    spread.\n\nJoselito became sick, so sick that no doctor could cure him. His body
    grew weak, and he died alone, far from the mountains.\n\nAs for Kapitan Lara,
    he was called back to Laguna when the flames of the revolution began to rise.
    The Filipino people have had enough. \n\nIn the chaos of the rebellion, Kapitan
    Lara met his end\u2014cut down by the very hands he once used for his own benefit.\n\nMaria
    Makiling was never seen again.\n\nSome say she returned to her mountain, never
    to come down again. Others say she walks in the forests, her presence a whisper
    in the wind.\n\nAnd when travelers lose their way near her home, they remember
    the diwata\u2019s great love and terrible curse that followed.\n\n\u2013 THE
    END \u2013\n"
